{
  "description": "A chat context snapshot a published release wrote, and what it becomes (#271), written by hand and compared byte for byte with the output of the v0.12.0 tag's own code for the same reads. Through v0.12.0 Go keyed a read's cache row from what its reader made of the arguments and ran the tool with the bytes the model wrote, so a row can hold the answer to arguments other than the ones its key names. A row is a key and an entry, so it cannot be told apart from a sound one. items[0] is version 1 with such rows: it still loads, and a read returns it as it was written. items[1] is the snapshot the current writer makes after restoring it: version 2, the same entries, ages and index rows, and no cache rows. Each of reads is one earlier read, the exact call its row would answer, and the answer that row holds: after a restore the call is run and answered for itself. A cold cache costs one more call per cached read and never a wrong answer. A published reader refuses version 2 with UnsupportedContextSnapshotVersion before it reads the body. context/readKeys.test.ts, go/context/read_arguments_test.go and go/adapter/eino/read_keys_test.go hold the code to it, and tests/test_contract.py derives every row.",
